@document.meta
title: "Devlog 2026-07-11: security pass"
slug: security-pass
date: 2026-07-11
summary: One govulncheck finding, then link validation, request limits, and pinned build inputs.
tags: [devlog, security, go]
draft: false
@end

I ran `govulncheck` on a quiet Saturday and it flagged `GO-2026-5856`, a reachable vulnerability in the `crypto/tls` package of Go 1.26.4. Bumping to 1.26.5 fixed it. While I had the hood open, I went looking for the next problem.

** Parser output is trusted HTML
The templates render parsed content with `templ.Raw`, so the parser decides what reaches the page. Links now go through a check before they become `<a href>`:
- relative paths, `http`, `https`, and `mailto` pass
- protocol-relative URLs like `//evil.example` fail
- any other scheme fails, `javascript:` included

A bad link fails the parse, and a failed parse stops the app at startup.

** Bounding requests
@table
| Limit | Value |
| - | - |
| search query | 256 bytes, then `400` |
| raw query string | 512 bytes, then `400` |
| request headers | 1 MiB, read within 5 seconds |
| writes | 30 seconds |
@end

I also fixed the gzip middleware. It used to flush an empty gzip stream on responses that should not have been compressed at all. And every response now carries HSTS and a Permissions-Policy that turns off the camera, microphone, and geolocation.

** Pinning the build
*** DONE Go 1.26.5 everywhere: `go.mod`, Dockerfile, CI
*** DONE Tailwind binary checked against its official SHA-256
*** DONE GitHub Actions pinned to commit SHAs
*** DONE test workflow token cut down to read access
*** DONE GoReleaser pinned to a major version

Every behavior change started as a failing test. I finished with `go test -race`, `go vet`, and a clean `govulncheck` run, all inside one afternoon.
